*Bellies up to the bar*

Well, it seems my unique playing style of nethacking obsessively for
two days, leaving town for a week, returning and hacking for another
four or five days, doing schoolwork, forgetting about Nethack for about
a month, discovering it again, moving into a new apartment, then
finishing my first ascension in a blazing 7-hour game was an absolute
success.  I've been playing for about three months, playing races and
classes more or less at random, although I started out playing Knights.
 What a mistake that turned out to be :)

Lathanel was never a lucky character.  The RNG seemed to be against me
the whole time - there were no randomly generated altars in the entire
dungeon, and only two shops.  Oddly enough, though, there seemed to be
a backwash of Bags of Holding - I spotted at least six throughout, and
I wasn't even looking that hard.  Also, as far as I can tell, there was
only one bones file generated, and the only /WoW I found was waiting in
the Castle.  The only magic lamp in the game was hanging out in a store
in mine Town, though, where I got my shiny blessed +2 grey dragon scale
mail.  On further consideration, though, lighting became a priority in
the late game - I should probably have saved it as a light source.  Oh
well - the scale mail served well.  Finally, the only artfact I found
generated randomly was Trollbane.  Whoopee.

He started with more or less average stats, the high point being a
strength of 13 and a dexterity of 17 but a charisma of only 8.  There
was a sink in the very first room, which I knew to keep away from from
past experiences.  Later, on one of the lower levels in the Mine, my
large dog named Sirius suddenly became a tame coutl named Sirius.
Needless to say, this was easily one of the best pets I had ever had
and was a major contribution to my early survival.  After Sirius ate
the Oracle I decided it was probably best to have some holy water on
hand, so I backtracked back down to Mine Town and had a harrowing
experience with the local priest, who decided that she hated elves,
coutls, and people converting her altar simultaneously.

The fountains seemed exceptionally dry in Mine Town - after siccing
Sirius on the town Watch, I tried dipping the spare potions I had
picked up along the way, and ended up with 2 diluted potions.  That
left me with a single potion of holy water that I was forced to use on
uncursing a nasty pair of gauntlets of dexterity.  I was forced to use
a spoiler to solve Sokoban, lacking the mental fortitude and the
patience to do it on my own.  It yielded nothing of value, though,
except for another bag of holding.  Sigh.  Ludios yielded absolutely
nothing of interest, except a blessed +4 pair of leather gloves, which
I hastily donned after ditching my old pair of rotting very burnt +0
gloves.

Things kept going along all right until about DL 20, where several
disasters decided to strike all at once.  First, due to a typographical
error I wound up eating a leprechaun corpse out wandering away from his
local Hall.  This left me with a very annoying case of teleportitis
that lasted until late-game - I had a c=3DoTC handy, so I put that on and
left it there until much later.  Luckily, I had a magic whistle handy
so I was able to keep Sirius nearby.  Unfortunately, Sirius, encouraged
by the success of his first polymorph trap, tried it again and ended up
as a very un-heroic violet fungus.  Sadly, he was eaten soon afterwards
by a jackal or something.  I'm not sure.  An egg tucked away in my bag
hatched rather unexpectedly into a baby orange dragon, lovingly named
Mandarin.  Mandarin served loyally for all of ten turns, after which he
was abruptly dispatched by a marauding band of Mordor orcs.

Things were decidedly difficult by the time I hit Medusa's place.
First, a gremlin had apparently found the area, and was multiplying as
fast as it could - fortunately, I was playing a chaotic, so it didn't
cause any real problems.  Lacking a means of levitation, water walking,
jumping or flying, I decided to backtrack and see what I could find.
After scouring the dungeon all the way back down to Mine Town I came up
with a crafty solution:  Using the same polymorph trap that had
dispatched my pet, I transformed into a female grey dragon (after
removing all my breakable euqipment first, of course).

Then it was a race with time to get back down to Medusa's, and over as
much water as I could before the spell wore off.  It did, but I was
already two steps onto land.  Ha!  This left me high and dry, and also
had a net benefit of two baby grey dragons, who were actually
suprisingly useful later on.  Perseus' statue left behind a bunch of
cool things, including a shield of reflection and a badly-needed pair
of water walking shoes, and Using these, I tracked my way over to
Medusa and took her head as a prize.

It was about this time that, having discovered a polymorph ring, a ring
of polymorph control, and an amulet of unchanging, I decided to
transform permenantly into an Aleax.  I was a little put off my the ~50
hp, but that was soon corrected using numerous !oFH and a cursed
genocide scroll.  Once I hit the castle, things started to look up - I
had a nearly full ascension kit cobbled together by hand, so I used the
/WoW there to wish for a blessed figurine of an Archon, 2 blessed
scrolls of charging, a blessed pair of +2 jumping boots and a blessed
magic marker.  All of these things were extremely useful in the game to
come.  The marker alone added at least 10 points to my AC, having
stocked blank scrolls for quite some time.  I named the Archon Sirius
II in honor of my dearly departed celestial serpent, and despite some
close calls with Stormbringer, we quickly became close partners.  The
fact that I had a spellbook of charm monsters handy probably helped :)

I worried greatly about facing the Wizard of Yendor.  I tracked through
the entire dungeon at least four times, stuffing useful things I could
find into a box (labeled PH4T 73WT) under a boulder on the altar in
Orcus' town.  It was at this point that I got most of the artifact
weapons listed below from the altar at Mine Town through a 10000 turn
sac-fest, zapping every spare /oCM and ?oCM I had found thus far.  This
was a major factor in gaining my high AC and HP (for an Archon) that I
left the game with.  Finally, a throne buried under a pile of useless
gems yielded a wish, which I used to get the ring.  Taking several deep
breaths, I woke him up and started running like hell.  Actually, Rodney
wasn't much of a bother.  The random curses were annoying, but my
gratuitous stock of holy water served well.  I made my way back down to
the VS, did the Invocation and dashed down the stairs.

Moloch's sanctum was one of the most fun bits of the entire game.  I
made sure to lock Sirius in the first room, and veeery carefully snuck
through the graveyard there using my intrinsic stealth and
invisibility.  Two purple worms were hanging out in the hall around the
temple itself, so I said "Hmm!" and slipped on my ring of conflict.
Many amusing gaseous emissions later, I used a /oSDD to locate the
secret entrance to the hidden sub-sub-basement temple, and told off
Moloch by slaying his priest with a luckily placed /oD found in the
temple itself.  Amulet of Yendor hot in hand, I dashed back up the
stairs (stopping to creep back through the graveyard - the undead never
suspected a thing) and started my long journey up the dungeon.

The journey was somewhat uneventful except for a few unnerving
experiences with purple worms, all whom seemed to be after Sirius.
Fortunately, I had dug paths through the worst of Gehemmon and through
a generous portion of the Dungeon, so I jumped as quickly as I could to
the top floor.  Me and Sirius stopped for a breather at the stairs,
dumped a pile of unnecessary equipment and headed on up, making sure we
had a pile of ?oDG handy.  The plane of Eath was fairly difficult - I
dig the trenches with Sirius following behind and keeping the way
clear.  Rodney showed up twice and made himself a nuisance, but I still
had plenty of charges in my /oD.  ("So, you thought you could kill me
that easily?" "Yep, still do." *ZAPPO*)  The portal on the plane of
fire was fortunately very close by - only 10 or 12 spaces away, and
across one lava flow.  Air was very difficult - I was as fast as the
Air Elementals, but there was lots of them and only one me.  I took the
better part of valor and dashed as quickly as I could through the thin
part of the clouds, letting Sirius deal with them.  Another plane down.
 Water was an awfully relaxing place, most of its denizens long since
genocided.  I dispatched the water elementals buzzing around there and
bobbed about until the portal swam into view.  The Astral Plane was a
highly worrying place, with several killer angels on the loose.  I had
thought to take off my =3DoC on the Plane of Water, but I put it back on
soon later - I figured that one rogue Angel of Mars would be easier to
kill than having tons of killer Angels of Moloch out for my blood.  The
Jumping boots were handy here, letting me fly past most of the
inhabitants except Pestilence, who took up a sickeningly defensive
stance in front of the doors.  Fortunately, my first choice turned out
to be the right one, and after calling Sirius to me and locking the
doors I arranged everything for a nice, scenic shot, and #offered the
Amulet.






